"# Chapter 10 — Lazy vs eager (the real Motoi story: `delay`/`force`)

In a module-having Scheme, this chapter would ask *when does `(import manners)` actually run the module's body — at parse time, or the first time you call a verb?* — and answer it with `:lazy` flags on the import form. **Motoi has no `import`, so it has no lazy-vs-eager loading of modules.** Its libraries are all eagerly present at boot; there is nothing to defer.

But the *idea* underneath — defer an expensive computation until you actually need its result, then remember it — is completely real in Motoi, and it has a clean, standard tool: **promises**, via `delay` and `force`. This chapter teaches that.

## 10.1 Libraries are eager; you never load them

The libraries are the eager part, and you get no say in it: string, list, hash-table, and random verbs are all present the instant Motoi starts. There is no `import` timing to reason about:

```scheme
(list (string-upcase \"ready\") (iota 3))
;; ⇒ (\"READY\" (0 1 2))
```

Both verbs were loaded before your first line ran. That is the whole of \"eager loading\" in Motoi: it already happened.

## 10.2 The lazy tool — `delay`

When *your own* program has an expensive value it might not need, wrap it in `delay`. That produces a **promise**: the body does not run until someone calls `force` on it.

```scheme
(define log '())
(define expensive
  (delay (begin (set! log (cons 'computed log)) 42)))

log            ; nothing computed yet
;; ⇒ ()
```

`expensive` is a promise. Its body has not run — `log` is still empty. This is deferral: you have described the computation without performing it.

## 10.3 `force` runs it once, then remembers

`force` runs a promise's body the first time and caches the result. Force it again and you get the cached value — the body does not run twice:

```scheme
(define count 0)
(define p (delay (begin (set! count (+ count 1)) (* 6 7))))

(list (force p)    ; runs the body, count becomes 1
      (force p)    ; returns cached value, body does NOT run again
      count)
;; ⇒ (42 42 1)
```

`count` is 1, not 2 — the second `force` used the memoized result. This is exactly the behavior a lazy import wanted: pay the cost once, on first use, then never again.

## 10.4 When lazy is right (the idea survives)

The other dialect's advice about *when* to be lazy translates directly to promises:

- **Lazy is right** when the value is expensive and you might not need it, or you want to pay its cost only on first use.
- **Lazy is wrong** when the computation might fail and you would rather find out at startup, or its side effects must happen up front, or you need predictable timing (a deferred cost shows up later, as a pause on first use).

The rule of thumb is the same: compute eagerly unless a value is proven expensive-and-maybe-unused.

## 10.5 Recording that you deferred, with Cortex

The other dialect logs `module-registered` then `module-loaded` events for a lazy import. Motoi has no such events, but if your program wants to note whether a deferred value was ever forced, Cortex can hold that fact:

```scheme
(define q (delay (begin (cortex/write 'q-forced #t) 99)))
(cortex/recall 'q-forced)     ; before forcing
```

Before you `force q`, the fact was never written; after, `(cortex/recall 'q-forced)` reflects it. That is how you'd tell, in Motoi, whether a deferred computation ever ran.

## 10.6 Precomputation vs on-demand

A related technique: if a value is expensive *and* always needed, do not defer it — precompute it once and keep it. A top-level `define` computes its value at load and holds it:

```scheme
(define big-table (map (lambda (n) (* n n)) (iota 10)))

(list-ref big-table 7)
;; ⇒ 49
```

`big-table` was computed once when the definition ran; every lookup after is cheap. Use `delay` when you *might not* need the value; use a plain `define` when you *always* will. That is the precompute-vs-defer decision, in Motoi's own terms.

## 10.7 Try it

Wrap an expensive-looking computation in `delay` and confirm its body does not run until you `force` it (watch a side-effect list stay empty, as in section 10.2). Force it twice and confirm the body ran only once (section 10.3). Then compare with a plain `define` that computes eagerly. You now have both tools: `delay`/`force` for maybe-needed values, `define` for always-needed ones — no `import` timing involved, because there is no import.

Chapter 11 covers circular imports in the other dialect — and why a batteries-included language has no import cycles to worry about. Turn the page.")
